var ALERTS={
 truck:["Truck parked inside","A space where vehicles park is usually classed as Group S-2 (parking) under the California Building Code, which can mean a fire-rated wall between it and other uses, plus other fire-code steps. The city/fire marshal decides. Look for units that already have a grade-level roll-up door, ask the broker/city early whether parking inside is allowed, and keep the bay away from welding and lasers."],
 plumbing:["New plumbing","Showers and added bathrooms need drain lines — often means cutting the concrete slab. Good item to ask the landlord to build as a lease concession."],
 weld:["Laser welder","Needs its own 240V circuit installed by a licensed electrician, a well-ventilated room, and argon or nitrogen shielding gas (bought separately). It's a Class 4 laser: wall it off or use laser curtains so nobody gets eye exposure. Keep it away from the truck bay and flammables."],
 co2:["Laser cutter fumes","CO2 laser cutters need fume exhaust ducted outside (or a filter unit). Put it on an exterior wall and ask the landlord about wall/roof penetrations."],
 gas:["Compressed gas","Argon/nitrogen cylinders must be chained upright, away from heat. The fire marshal may ask how much compressed gas is stored."],
 commercial:["Commercial kitchen equipment = inspections","Commercial cooking gear generally needs a Type I exhaust hood with fire suppression and city/fire inspections. If food is ever sold or served to the public, an Orange County Health Care Agency food-facility permit too. Residential appliances avoid most of this (matches what his AI said)."],
 public:["Classes = public use","Teaching classes means members of the public on site, which can change the building's occupancy (exits, restrooms, accessibility, sprinklers). Confirm with the city before signing if classes are likely."],
 ceiling:["Ceiling height","Ask every listing for 'clear height'. A 10 ft rim with real shooting arc wants roughly 18–20 ft clear; batting and tennis need ~12 ft+."],
 garden:["Water & humidity","Hydroponics needs a water source and floor drain nearby and adds humidity — keep it away from electronics and the shop."],
 dust:["Wood dust","Run a dust collector and keep the woodshop separated (wall or curtain) from the arcade, sims and lounge so dust stays out of electronics."],
 hvac:["Cooling","He wants it not hot. Many warehouses only heat (or use evaporative coolers) in the open area. Cooling 10k sq ft is a large electrical load — a prime concession to ask for, and something to verify on every tour."],
 fumes:["Fumes","Resin, solvents, glazes and finishes give off fumes. Plan an extractor or a window/exhaust fan, and keep it away from the lounge."],
 ev:["EV charging","A Level 2 charger needs a 240V circuit (often 50–60 A). Check the panel has room before planning one."],
 threephase:["3-phase power","This machine usually needs 3-phase power (208V or 480V). Many small industrial units only have single-phase; ask on every tour. A phase converter is the fallback."],
 spray:["Spray finishing","Paint and powder spraying needs a proper booth with exhaust, and the fire marshal will want to see it. Keep it away from welding, grinding and lasers."],
 notyet:["Not released yet","Not available yet — dimensions and power are placeholders."]
};
/* Library rules:
   - Every object takes floor space. Things that sit on a counter, hang from the ceiling or mount on a wall are folded
     into the floor object they belong to (e.g. "Base cabinet + microwave", "Projector + screen wall", "EV parking bay").
   - Names never state a size (no feet, inches or seat counts): people resize objects. One object per kind of thing,
     not one per size; tiers (entry / mid / pro) only where the machine itself differs.
   - Generic, representative objects only, no brands; use tiers (entry / mid / pro) when size or cost varies a lot.
   - Retired objects: map to a replacement in ALIAS, or keep them with hide:1 so old layouts still draw them. */
